Pain — experience, tissue signals, and context (@med-pain: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @medicine, #source @med-source-pain, #medicine @med-history) { r_7VX3R5W6XHV21R7EH2N70PBP35
**Read:** [Pain terminology — IASP](../sources/Pain-terminology.lingua#r_4V7XHP0R3Y4QQVN47RD1YEVZCV), especially the distinction between pain and nociception and the definitions of nociceptive, neuropathic, and nociplastic pain.

Pain/dor is an experienced event affected by biological, psychological, and social factors. Nociception/nocicepção refers to neural processing of potentially harmful stimuli. They are related processes with different meanings. This does not make pain imaginary or justify dismissing it.

Tissue injury can produce pain, but pain intensity is not a direct measurement of the size of an injury. Sleep, prior experience, threat, and other factors can influence the experience. Neuropathic pain concerns a lesion or disease affecting the somatosensory nervous system; the word cannot be inferred merely from severe or burning pain.

A useful description records site, onset, course, qualities, associated symptoms, what changes it, and effects on daily activities. Local and referred pain describe where pain is experienced in relation to its source. These distinctions will matter at the shoulder.

**Try:** rewrite 'my shoulder is damaged because it hurts a lot' as separate observations and hypotheses. State what an anatomical image could contribute and what it could not establish about the experience.

**Continue:** [History, signs, symptoms, and clinical questions](History-and-examination.lingua#r_7HPNE5X66CBS1BE9941J5DN17Y).

